what you do at Google?

00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:13.550
SHEROL CHEN: Yeah.

00:04:13.550 --> 00:04:15.210
So I'm an advocate.

00:04:15.210 --> 00:04:16.850
I work in machine learning.

00:04:16.850 --> 00:04:20.450
Specifically I am working on an
awesome product called AutoML

00:04:20.450 --> 00:04:22.580
Tables, and what
it really does is

00:04:22.580 --> 00:04:24.950
it takes the concept
of machine learning

00:04:24.950 --> 00:04:28.083
and really breaks it down
to what data you have

00:04:28.083 --> 00:04:30.000
and what kind of predictions
you want to make.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:32.002
And these are just
really interesting tools

00:04:32.002 --> 00:04:34.210
that keep emerging, it seems
like, month after month.

00:04:34.210 --> 00:04:37.490
So I've been in machine learning
and AI for quite some time

00:04:37.490 --> 00:04:40.250
and served on a bunch of
different teams and roles

00:04:40.250 --> 00:04:41.637
in my time at Google.

00:04:41.637 --> 00:04:43.970
JON: That's awesome, and
you've done some really, really

00:04:43.970 --> 00:04:46.670
interesting things, like I see
that you've taught a class.

00:04:46.670 --> 00:04:48.842
So you want to talk about
that a little bit and more

00:04:48.842 --> 00:04:50.300
about your background
a little bit?

00:04:50.300 --> 00:04:51.050
SHEROL CHEN: Yeah.

00:04:51.050 --> 00:04:54.860
So I would say that I
was born on a cold winter

00:04:54.860 --> 00:04:56.120
night or something like that.

00:04:58.760 --> 00:05:00.680
I think a really
defining moment for me

00:05:00.680 --> 00:05:04.830
was when I first started
playing video games,

00:05:04.830 --> 00:05:07.730
and I had this hero's
journey experience

00:05:07.730 --> 00:05:11.300
that really got me through
the ups and downs of life

00:05:11.300 --> 00:05:14.180
and led me into
pursuing technology.

00:05:14.180 --> 00:05:15.980
It really stirred
a curiosity in me

00:05:15.980 --> 00:05:18.520
as to how do we
utilize these tools.

00:05:18.520 --> 00:05:20.930
And so my background
really took off,

00:05:20.930 --> 00:05:23.660
I think, in undergrad
where you were around all

00:05:23.660 --> 00:05:25.250
of these professors
and researchers,

00:05:25.250 --> 00:05:28.470
and I took a particular interest
in artificial intelligence,

00:05:28.470 --> 00:05:30.920
specifically because I felt
like games had improved

00:05:30.920 --> 00:05:32.780
so much in graphics,
but not so much

00:05:32.780 --> 00:05:34.700
in the logic behind the game.

00:05:34.700 --> 00:05:37.560
And then I went
on and did my PhD

00:05:37.560 --> 00:05:41.270
with leading expert in AI for
storytelling, Michael Matias,

00:05:41.270 --> 00:05:44.360
and from there I thought
I would be in academia

00:05:44.360 --> 00:05:46.440
for a good chunk of my life.

00:05:46.440 --> 00:05:49.310
And I did a lot of teaching
in the midst of going

00:05:49.310 --> 00:05:51.440
through grad school,
but then I also

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:54.680
had an opportunity to work at
Google as a software engineer

00:05:54.680 --> 00:05:57.020
and saw kind of
a whole new world

00:05:57.020 --> 00:05:59.120
opened up and decided
to come to Google.

00:05:59.120 --> 00:06:02.340
And I ended up in the right
place at the right time.

00:06:02.340 --> 00:06:04.820
I was at Google Brain
when Sundar announced

00:06:04.820 --> 00:06:06.530
that we were going AI first.

00:06:06.530 --> 00:06:09.380
When I was at
Stanford teaching it

00:06:09.380 --> 00:06:12.050
was a time when a lot of
people were interested

00:06:12.050 --> 00:06:14.980
in AI, so I was putting together
the curriculum that was going

00:06:14.980 --> 00:06:16.850
to get used around the world.

00:06:16.850 --> 00:06:20.600
It just is something that I've
always felt an affinity for.

00:06:20.600 --> 00:06:21.350
AJA: Wow.

00:06:21.350 --> 00:06:22.830
That is super cool.

00:06:22.830 --> 00:06:23.720
Did I hear right?

00:06:23.720 --> 00:06:27.020
You partly got into AI because
you thought that NPCs and video

00:06:27.020 --> 00:06:28.340
games should be smarter?

00:06:28.340 --> 00:06:31.370
SHEROL CHEN: Yes, absolutely.

00:06:31.370 --> 00:06:35.750
If you looked at Final Fantasy
1 and then Final Fantasy XV,

00:06:35.750 --> 00:06:39.200
you'd see this huge
difference in the feel

00:06:39.200 --> 00:06:42.560
and the environment and
the sound and scene.

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:44.433
But when you interact,
I just remember--

00:06:44.433 --> 00:06:46.100
I think for me, it
was Final Fantasy X--

00:06:46.100 --> 00:06:48.315
and you would look at
the maps of the world

00:06:48.315 --> 00:06:49.190
and it's like a tube.

00:06:49.190 --> 00:06:51.530
You're just going
down a pipe of point

00:06:51.530 --> 00:06:53.360
to point to point to point.

00:06:53.360 --> 00:06:56.610
On your way there, it's
fantastical and amazing,

00:06:56.610 --> 00:07:00.470
but what about the actual
universe itself, you know?

00:07:00.470 --> 00:07:01.220
JON: Awesome.

00:07:01.220 --> 00:07:02.810
AJA: I think a lot
of our listeners

00:07:02.810 --> 00:07:05.500
probably know about AI and
have a good idea what it is,

00:07:05.500 --> 00:07:07.250
but a lot of people
define it differently,

00:07:07.250 --> 00:07:09.165
so what do you
consider AI, Sherol,

00:07:09.165 --> 00:07:11.540
so we can all be working from
the same set of definitions

00:07:11.540 --> 00:07:12.320
today?

00:07:12.320 --> 00:07:15.710
SHEROL CHEN: That is definitely
a question that comes up a lot.

00:07:15.710 --> 00:07:18.320
I've been interested
and studying and working

00:07:18.320 --> 00:07:19.940
AI for the last
10 years, so I've

00:07:19.940 --> 00:07:24.620
seen the shift in how
people think about AI.

00:07:24.620 --> 00:07:28.400
I would say that it really
stems from the '50s and '60s.

00:07:28.400 --> 00:07:30.080
That definition
is the one that I

00:07:30.080 --> 00:07:32.510
feel is historical
and has meaning,

00:07:32.510 --> 00:07:35.510
and there was that Dartmouth
summer school where

00:07:35.510 --> 00:07:36.845
the term AI was even coined.

00:07:36.845 --> 00:07:38.900
So I think that there's
definitely historical

00:07:38.900 --> 00:07:41.990
meaning that I think is
valuable to consider.

00:07:41.990 --> 00:07:44.390
I also feel like
Alan Turing's paper

00:07:44.390 --> 00:07:46.060
he wrote in the
early '50s about can

00:07:46.060 --> 00:07:50.270
machines think was also posing
these really important thought

00:07:50.270 --> 00:07:54.230
experiments as to what do we
mean when we say intelligence.

00:07:54.230 --> 00:07:56.660
Now I think when I've
taught, whether it's

00:07:56.660 --> 00:07:59.840
an 11-year-old or
a grad student, AI,

00:07:59.840 --> 00:08:03.100
the real question that you can
really relate anybody to is

00:08:03.100 --> 00:08:04.430
what does it mean to be human.

00:08:04.430 --> 00:08:06.230
Or a more specific
question is, what

00:08:06.230 --> 00:08:08.450
are the things that machines
do better than humans,

00:08:08.450 --> 00:08:11.300
and what are the things that
humans do better than machines?

00:08:11.300 --> 00:08:13.160
And that has always
been kind of a standard

00:08:13.160 --> 00:08:16.610
of how we develop and measure
artificial intelligence.

00:08:16.610 --> 00:08:18.740
So then when you think
about things that humans do,

00:08:18.740 --> 00:08:21.320
we can see, smell, hear, think.

00:08:21.320 --> 00:08:22.340
We have language.

00:08:22.340 --> 00:08:23.270
We're creative.

00:08:23.270 --> 00:08:24.350
We're visual.

00:08:24.350 --> 00:08:27.140
All of these things being
captured by technology

00:08:27.140 --> 00:08:29.610
creates these different
areas of research,

00:08:29.610 --> 00:08:32.600
whether we're talking robotics
or natural language or machine

00:08:32.600 --> 00:08:33.150
learning.

00:08:33.150 --> 00:08:36.233
And machine learning has
really taken center stage

00:08:36.233 --> 00:08:37.400
in the last couple of years.

00:08:37.400 --> 00:08:38.775
I think it's really
the move that

00:08:38.775 --> 00:08:42.179
made Sundar announce that
we were going AI first,

00:08:42.179 --> 00:08:44.600
because of all the advancements
that we were able to do.

00:08:44.600 --> 00:08:48.080
And that happened as a result
of all of the increased ability

00:08:48.080 --> 00:08:50.870
to compute, the amount of
data that we had, and just

00:08:50.870 --> 00:08:52.680
the algorithms working better.

00:08:52.680 --> 00:08:55.460
So AI has historical
meaningfulness.

00:08:55.460 --> 00:08:57.630
It also has different
depending on the context

00:08:57.630 --> 00:08:58.610
that you're looking at.

00:08:58.610 --> 00:09:02.210
For me, my interest for AI was
believability and expressivity,

00:09:02.210 --> 00:09:05.880
so how we use AI to
extend human's ability

00:09:05.880 --> 00:09:08.430
to communicate and express
themselves in story.

00:09:08.430 --> 00:09:10.800
And I think machine
learning and deep learning

00:09:10.800 --> 00:09:13.590
has been, in the last couple of
years, the big emphasis of what

00:09:13.590 --> 00:09:17.020
has shown to be more effective
than ever had been before.

00:09:17.020 --> 00:09:18.910
So if you're talking
about AI, I think

00:09:18.910 --> 00:09:20.910
it's worth reading the
history and understanding

00:09:20.910 --> 00:09:22.090
where it comes from.

00:09:22.090 --> 00:09:24.300
I think it's really
important to not just think

00:09:24.300 --> 00:09:26.153
of AI as being
something really cool

00:09:26.153 --> 00:09:28.320
but really understand the
problems that AI is trying

00:09:28.320 --> 00:09:31.620
to solve, in which case you'll
get a different angle of what

00:09:31.620 --> 00:09:32.320
AI is.

00:09:32.320 --> 00:09:34.205
And finally, if you're
thinking about AI

00:09:34.205 --> 00:09:35.580
and what the buzz
is about today,

00:09:35.580 --> 00:09:37.410
it's about deep
learning and machine

00:09:37.410 --> 00:09:40.650
learning because of the efficacy
of these new advancements

00:09:40.650 --> 00:09:41.800
in technology.

00:09:41.800 --> 00:09:43.805
AJA: So I think I know
the answer to this,

00:09:43.805 --> 00:09:45.180
but I'm not
confident, so can you

00:09:45.180 --> 00:09:46.962
tell me what deep learning is?

00:09:46.962 --> 00:09:47.670
SHEROL CHEN: Yes.

00:09:47.670 --> 00:09:50.010
So machine learning
is something--

00:09:50.010 --> 00:09:54.180
Alan Turing, back in the 1950s,
he said, machine learning-- can

00:09:54.180 --> 00:09:54.930
machines think?

00:09:54.930 --> 00:09:57.670
Can machines actually acquire
knowledge on their own?

00:09:57.670 --> 00:10:00.350
That's some of the
earliest published concepts

00:10:00.350 --> 00:10:01.350
around machine learning.

00:10:01.350 --> 00:10:04.530
I think one of the earliest
programs that was actually made

00:10:04.530 --> 00:10:06.720
was actually, a lot of
these early AI programmers

00:10:06.720 --> 00:10:09.120
were psychologists
or they were in

00:10:09.120 --> 00:10:12.660
these interdisciplinary roles
of technology and psychology.

00:10:12.660 --> 00:10:15.840
And I believe that the first
machine learning program was

00:10:15.840 --> 00:10:19.050
actually a checkers program
where somebody was wondering,

00:10:19.050 --> 00:10:21.960
how could I build
checkers program that

00:10:21.960 --> 00:10:23.130
could beat me at checkers?

00:10:23.130 --> 00:10:25.590
And so it would apply
some sort of reinforcement

00:10:25.590 --> 00:10:29.382
learning to be able to figure
out how to win at checkers.

00:10:29.382 --> 00:10:31.090
That actually happened
not that much-- it

00:10:31.090 --> 00:10:32.970
must have been a decade
later or something

00:10:32.970 --> 00:10:34.090
for which that happened.

00:10:34.090 --> 00:10:36.160
So it wasn't something
that was in our recent

00:10:36.160 --> 00:10:38.040
last five to 10 years.

00:10:38.040 --> 00:10:39.840
Now the problem is
that there's been

00:10:39.840 --> 00:10:43.710
a lot of randomness and
unpredictability of having

00:10:43.710 --> 00:10:46.380
the machine just kind of
process information on its own

00:10:46.380 --> 00:10:48.870
without the guidance
and the formalities

00:10:48.870 --> 00:10:51.150
that we lay out, like the
rule-based systems that we

00:10:51.150 --> 00:10:52.510
typically use.

00:10:52.510 --> 00:10:54.990
I think, hand-in-hand,
there's an intuition of,

00:10:54.990 --> 00:10:57.740
oh, we can kind of guide the
logic of how the machine is

00:10:57.740 --> 00:10:58.680
going to break down.

00:10:58.680 --> 00:11:01.740
What's the best decision
given a particular situation?

00:11:01.740 --> 00:11:03.340
There's all these
different states,

00:11:03.340 --> 00:11:05.550
and this clearly is
the more ideal state

00:11:05.550 --> 00:11:07.395
and feed all the
information for that.

00:11:07.395 --> 00:11:08.520
So that's machine learning.

00:11:08.520 --> 00:11:11.370
And again, not everybody agrees
this is the way it works,

00:11:11.370 --> 00:11:15.090
but there's AI, and then within
AI, a part of intelligence

00:11:15.090 --> 00:11:18.240
is the ability to learn, for
which machine learning is just

00:11:18.240 --> 00:11:21.990
the computer acquiring its
own understanding of some sort

00:11:21.990 --> 00:11:24.240
of process or decision.

00:11:24.240 --> 00:11:26.880
So with deep learning
and machine learning,

00:11:26.880 --> 00:11:28.830
there is this concept
of neural networks.

00:11:28.830 --> 00:11:31.200
And neural networks
are not the only way--

00:11:31.200 --> 00:11:33.300
this idea of a
computational neuron

00:11:33.300 --> 00:11:35.530
is not the only way
machines can learn, right?

00:11:35.530 --> 00:11:36.480
There's other ways.

00:11:36.480 --> 00:11:38.390
Decision tree
learning, for example.

00:11:38.390 --> 00:11:40.140
But with neural networks,
you're basically

00:11:40.140 --> 00:11:41.510
balancing a bunch of weights.

00:11:41.510 --> 00:11:43.620
You're given
information, and it goes

00:11:43.620 --> 00:11:47.070
through these mathematical
calculations in neurons

00:11:47.070 --> 00:11:48.840
that balance a bunch
of weights to create

00:11:48.840 --> 00:11:51.810
this model, or this
mathematical representation,

00:11:51.810 --> 00:11:54.780
of how, given input
A, you get output B.

00:11:54.780 --> 00:11:57.240
How it gets deep is
that it's very limited

00:11:57.240 --> 00:11:59.880
if you stick to these
purely [INAUDIBLE]

00:11:59.880 --> 00:12:01.800
or linear operations.

00:12:01.800 --> 00:12:04.620
Deep learning really introduces
the ability for the computer

00:12:04.620 --> 00:12:07.890
to remove and hide some
of that information

00:12:07.890 --> 00:12:09.720
and pick and choose
what it's going to do,

00:12:09.720 --> 00:12:13.590
and it introduces an even
bigger layer of obscurity,

00:12:13.590 --> 00:12:15.340
but it gives the
machine more power.

00:12:15.340 --> 00:12:18.120
What it does come at the
cost of is understandability

00:12:18.120 --> 00:12:21.120
of what's actually happening
when it makes that decision.

00:12:21.120 --> 00:12:23.580
There's AI, there's
machine learning,

00:12:23.580 --> 00:12:25.800
and then there's deep
learning, and then within that

00:12:25.800 --> 00:12:29.460
is neural networks, and then the
hidden layers are the ones that

00:12:29.460 --> 00:12:31.298
make it a deep learning system.

00:12:31.298 --> 00:12:31.840
JON: Awesome.

00:12:31.840 --> 00:12:33.030
You said something
really interesting

00:12:33.030 --> 00:12:34.072
that I want to ask about.

00:12:34.072 --> 00:12:38.640
You mentioned that, early on,
AI was pretty much psychiatrists

00:12:38.640 --> 00:12:42.390
and someone in computer science,
like the intermediary track.

00:12:42.390 --> 00:12:46.680
But I'm curious, for
AI and ML, where's

00:12:46.680 --> 00:12:50.790
that line drawn between
what decisions a human makes

00:12:50.790 --> 00:12:53.880
and the type of models that
you have to actually build

00:12:53.880 --> 00:12:55.440
and what the machine
actually does?

00:12:55.440 --> 00:12:56.190
SHEROL CHEN: Yeah.

00:12:56.190 --> 00:12:58.840
I think that humans
are imperfect,

00:12:58.840 --> 00:13:01.672
so when I asked the question
in a classroom, what

00:13:01.672 --> 00:13:03.630
are things that humans
do better than machines?

00:13:03.630 --> 00:13:05.797
What are things that machines
do better than humans?

00:13:05.797 --> 00:13:09.480
And then a common
debate is driving.

00:13:09.480 --> 00:13:12.698
So who's going to be a
better driver, or what

00:13:12.698 --> 00:13:13.990
is going to be a better driver?

00:13:13.990 --> 00:13:16.920
Is it going to be the human or
is it going to be the computer?

00:13:16.920 --> 00:13:18.828
And you're kind of torn.

00:13:18.828 --> 00:13:20.370
There are a lot of
things that humans

00:13:20.370 --> 00:13:22.590
can do that we take for
granted, but there are also

00:13:22.590 --> 00:13:24.263
just clear weaknesses
that we have,

00:13:24.263 --> 00:13:26.430
right, like when we're
tired, when we're inebriated.

00:13:26.430 --> 00:13:28.860
There's different
things that prevent us

00:13:28.860 --> 00:13:31.410
from functioning
at full capacity.

00:13:31.410 --> 00:13:34.620
And so a lot of our traffic
laws are built around this idea

00:13:34.620 --> 00:13:38.700
that you'd have all your correct
sensory functions in order.

00:13:38.700 --> 00:13:40.950
When we think about how we
create policies for traffic

00:13:40.950 --> 00:13:43.380
laws, for example,
they exist that you

00:13:43.380 --> 00:13:45.180
need to be able to
see, for example, to be

00:13:45.180 --> 00:13:46.870
able to drive a car.

00:13:46.870 --> 00:13:49.320
But there is some
margin of, well,

00:13:49.320 --> 00:13:53.040
you don't have to have exact,
perfect, immaculate 20/20

00:13:53.040 --> 00:13:53.790
vision.

00:13:53.790 --> 00:13:55.958
You can have a little
bit less than that,

00:13:55.958 --> 00:13:58.250
let's say, and then there's
some amount less than that.

00:13:58.250 --> 00:14:00.930
And all of that needs to be
researched and considered

00:14:00.930 --> 00:14:04.103
to be able to protect the
safety of society as a whole.

00:14:04.103 --> 00:14:06.270
But I don't think that's
an easy question to answer,

00:14:06.270 --> 00:14:08.200
because when I talk
about self-driving cars,

00:14:08.200 --> 00:14:11.850
if we talk about it in Silicon
Valley or the Bay Area,

00:14:11.850 --> 00:14:15.990
we're thinking like highway
280 where it's wide and open

00:14:15.990 --> 00:14:17.580
and the lines are very clear.

00:14:17.580 --> 00:14:20.280
But I've taught in
India before, and when

00:14:20.280 --> 00:14:24.160
I show them the video of a
self-driving car in California,

00:14:24.160 --> 00:14:26.283
yeah, that does
seem like terrain

00:14:26.283 --> 00:14:27.450
that a machine can navigate.

00:14:27.450 --> 00:14:31.350
If you show them the streets
of some of the places in India,

00:14:31.350 --> 00:14:33.360
I just don't even know
what kind of input--

00:14:33.360 --> 00:14:36.090
you'd just have to have a
very fast, reactionary state

00:14:36.090 --> 00:14:37.420
of processing.

00:14:37.420 --> 00:14:39.030
It's a complicated
system, but I don't

00:14:39.030 --> 00:14:40.420
think that it's anything new.

00:14:40.420 --> 00:14:42.750
I don't think that how
societies function up to now--

00:14:42.750 --> 00:14:44.820
we've always had new technology.

00:14:44.820 --> 00:14:46.350
How did we introduce the car?

00:14:46.350 --> 00:14:48.390
How did we introduce
the printing press?

00:14:48.390 --> 00:14:50.100
All of these things
have required

00:14:50.100 --> 00:14:52.980
us to adjust the way we
understand and see the world.

00:14:52.980 --> 00:14:54.560
JON: That's super insightful.

00:14:54.560 --> 00:14:55.560
I have another question.

00:14:55.560 --> 00:14:58.183
You mentioned
self-driving cars and we

00:14:58.183 --> 00:14:59.850
started talking about
robotics, but what

00:14:59.850 --> 00:15:03.870
about the rest of technologies,
such as education and medicine,

00:15:03.870 --> 00:15:04.470
potentially.

00:15:04.470 --> 00:15:07.010
What impact would AI
and ML have on that?

00:15:07.010 --> 00:15:07.760
SHEROL CHEN: Yeah.

00:15:07.760 --> 00:15:12.490
So I think that that word,
impact, is very appropriate.

00:15:12.490 --> 00:15:16.440
I was recently asked to be
a judge for the Google's AI

00:15:16.440 --> 00:15:21.180
Impact Challenge where a lot of
grant money was awarded to 20

00:15:21.180 --> 00:15:24.150
recipients and they all came
up with proposals on what kind

00:15:24.150 --> 00:15:26.640
of impact AI could have--
social good impact--

00:15:26.640 --> 00:15:27.550
in the world.

00:15:27.550 --> 00:15:29.535
And one of the things
you brought up, medical.

00:15:29.535 --> 00:15:34.080
A lot of these grants were
awarded to medical use cases.

00:15:34.080 --> 00:15:36.070
Being able to pass
through medical records,

00:15:36.070 --> 00:15:37.200
which has also evolved.

00:15:37.200 --> 00:15:38.350
We have all this data.

00:15:38.350 --> 00:15:39.100
How do we use it?

00:15:39.100 --> 00:15:41.250
How do we use it to do
preventative things?

00:15:41.250 --> 00:15:44.190
How do we use it to help,
not just humans, but animals,

00:15:44.190 --> 00:15:46.520
like agriculture technology.

00:15:46.520 --> 00:15:48.810
A lot of people in
areas where they're

00:15:48.810 --> 00:15:52.170
focusing on food
and plant livelihood

00:15:52.170 --> 00:15:55.170
are able to now use technology
to be able to figure out

00:15:55.170 --> 00:15:59.460
how to best cultivate resources
for maximizing food and health

00:15:59.460 --> 00:16:01.270
and safety and things like that.

00:16:01.270 --> 00:16:04.080
And the other thing is that,
now that we have Android phones,

00:16:04.080 --> 00:16:07.620
we have the ability to do a
lot of things with the camera

00:16:07.620 --> 00:16:09.250
and we have on device.

00:16:09.250 --> 00:16:11.700
We could build models
and train them on device.

00:16:11.700 --> 00:16:15.390
We can take this relatively
inexpensive medical device

00:16:15.390 --> 00:16:18.930
through the phone and
go to areas in the world

00:16:18.930 --> 00:16:21.210
where you can diagnose
infections and be

00:16:21.210 --> 00:16:24.233
able to prescribe the right
antibiotics or medicines.

00:16:24.233 --> 00:16:25.650
So these are things
that you would

00:16:25.650 --> 00:16:28.560
be able to multiply the
abilities of these high

00:16:28.560 --> 00:16:30.510
in demand skill
sets, like medical,

00:16:30.510 --> 00:16:34.290
but also other things like
the environment or agriculture

00:16:34.290 --> 00:16:37.830
or social services, like
education, and the ability

00:16:37.830 --> 00:16:40.953
to have coverage in areas
that we didn't have before.

00:16:40.953 --> 00:16:42.870
JON: So you mentioned
the AI Impact Challenge.

00:16:42.870 --> 00:16:43.950
I happened to see--

00:16:43.950 --> 00:16:47.070
I'm not sure which year this
challenge has been going on,

00:16:47.070 --> 00:16:49.340
but they announced the
winners at Google I/O,

00:16:49.340 --> 00:16:50.410
which was really awesome.

00:16:50.410 --> 00:16:52.320
I think you mentioned that you
had something to do with that.

00:16:52.320 --> 00:16:53.310
You were a judge.

00:16:53.310 --> 00:16:56.670
But I'm curious, when you
think about Impact and AI,

00:16:56.670 --> 00:16:59.250
what about the ethical
parts of AI and ML?

00:16:59.250 --> 00:17:00.990
Because I would
imagine that some

00:17:00.990 --> 00:17:03.907
of these contestants or
participants in the challenge

00:17:03.907 --> 00:17:05.490
would have to think
about these things

00:17:05.490 --> 00:17:08.880
when they're actually building
out any of their solutions

00:17:08.880 --> 00:17:10.218
to real world problems.

00:17:10.218 --> 00:17:11.760
So I'm just curious,
what do you have

00:17:11.760 --> 00:17:14.473
to say about using
AI and ML ethically?

00:17:14.473 --> 00:17:16.890
SHEROL CHEN: That was definitely
one of the considerations

00:17:16.890 --> 00:17:17.980
when reviewing.

00:17:17.980 --> 00:17:21.780
And often you get asked, whether
you're speaking at a conference

00:17:21.780 --> 00:17:25.290
or at an event, that people
will ask, how can I use AI?

00:17:25.290 --> 00:17:27.180
We work with enterprise
customers a lot,

00:17:27.180 --> 00:17:29.700
and they're using AI to
help better their business

00:17:29.700 --> 00:17:32.368
or to help make better choices
in directions that they'd

00:17:32.368 --> 00:17:32.910
like to take.

00:17:32.910 --> 00:17:35.850
So definitely AI
is a powerful tool

00:17:35.850 --> 00:17:37.950
to be used in these processes.

00:17:37.950 --> 00:17:40.890
When I was looking at these
applicants, a lot of this stuff

00:17:40.890 --> 00:17:41.610
came to mind.

00:17:41.610 --> 00:17:43.860
I think, especially when
you're dealing with something

00:17:43.860 --> 00:17:45.470
medically related,
yeah, it would

00:17:45.470 --> 00:17:47.880
be great to get an
awesome medical dataset,

00:17:47.880 --> 00:17:50.490
but how do we protect
the people that are

00:17:50.490 --> 00:17:52.500
being represented in this data?

00:17:52.500 --> 00:17:57.540
And then say you're using
Google Assistant to check out

00:17:57.540 --> 00:18:00.390
how someone's mental
state of being is.

00:18:00.390 --> 00:18:03.480
How do you make sure that
that is not infringing

00:18:03.480 --> 00:18:04.890
on that person's privacy?

00:18:04.890 --> 00:18:06.660
So these are things
that really do

00:18:06.660 --> 00:18:09.480
need to be not
reactively thought about,

00:18:09.480 --> 00:18:11.350
but proactively thought about.

00:18:11.350 --> 00:18:12.600
And so that's something that--

00:18:12.600 --> 00:18:14.880
I think it's arguable that
we're kind of keeping up

00:18:14.880 --> 00:18:16.590
with the move of
technology, but I also

00:18:16.590 --> 00:18:18.173
feel like there's
always more that can

00:18:18.173 --> 00:18:19.890
be done in those regards.

00:18:19.890 --> 00:18:22.410
AJA: So what kind of tools
are available to people

00:18:22.410 --> 00:18:24.010
who want to start
learning about AI

00:18:24.010 --> 00:18:26.400
or start trying to train
models, and what kinds of tools

00:18:26.400 --> 00:18:28.103
do you work on at Google?

00:18:28.103 --> 00:18:28.770
SHEROL CHEN: OK.

00:18:28.770 --> 00:18:31.140
Of course there's buzz around
AI, and people are like,

00:18:31.140 --> 00:18:34.120
it's just kind of the hype
of the moment or something.

00:18:34.120 --> 00:18:36.870
But I think it's true that
this technology is going

00:18:36.870 --> 00:18:38.540
to continue to
improve, and it's going

00:18:38.540 --> 00:18:40.692
to be a big part of society.

00:18:40.692 --> 00:18:42.900
And I think similar, maybe
not in the exact same way,

00:18:42.900 --> 00:18:46.290
as how the printing press
changed baseline skill sets

00:18:46.290 --> 00:18:47.200
that people needed.

00:18:47.200 --> 00:18:51.600
I think if you look at reading
literacy 100 years ago,

00:18:51.600 --> 00:18:55.310
it was probably like 90%
of the world couldn't read,

00:18:55.310 --> 00:18:56.830
and now it's flipped.

00:18:56.830 --> 00:18:59.290
Now 90% of the world can read.

00:18:59.290 --> 00:19:00.850
So I think that if
we can stay on top

00:19:00.850 --> 00:19:03.250
of what's going on in
the world in technology,

00:19:03.250 --> 00:19:04.670
I think that's very important.

00:19:04.670 --> 00:19:08.050
So for me personally, I try
to read about what's going on,

00:19:08.050 --> 00:19:11.770
what other businesses are doing,
what other research groups are

00:19:11.770 --> 00:19:12.850
working on.

00:19:12.850 --> 00:19:14.710
You try to keep track
of the big conferences

00:19:14.710 --> 00:19:18.280
like [INAUDIBLE]
and ICLR and ICML.

00:19:18.280 --> 00:19:22.720
I tweet a lot, just a lot of the
current, interesting highlights

00:19:22.720 --> 00:19:24.850
of the moment in
machine learning and AI.

00:19:24.850 --> 00:19:27.700
But outside of just
keeping up as to what

00:19:27.700 --> 00:19:30.790
is being presented through
conferences and media,

00:19:30.790 --> 00:19:35.572
I think a really good resource
for learning the basics of it

00:19:35.572 --> 00:19:37.780
and just understanding it
from a technical standpoint

00:19:37.780 --> 00:19:40.210
is the machine
learning crash course.

00:19:40.210 --> 00:19:42.040
I've gone through it twice.

00:19:42.040 --> 00:19:45.290
One time I went through
it in a guided classroom,

00:19:45.290 --> 00:19:47.050
and then I just went
through it by myself

00:19:47.050 --> 00:19:49.720
and just looked at
every unit and worked

00:19:49.720 --> 00:19:52.110
through a lot of the
basics and fundamentals

00:19:52.110 --> 00:19:53.470
through that crash course.

00:19:53.470 --> 00:19:55.960
And it uses
TensorFlow and Python,

00:19:55.960 --> 00:19:58.400
so you have to have
a baseline in that.

00:19:58.400 --> 00:20:01.750
But I thought it was one of
the best, most up to date

00:20:01.750 --> 00:20:05.860
representation of what AI is
being most used right now.

00:20:05.860 --> 00:20:07.590
Now, there's other things.

00:20:07.590 --> 00:20:10.090
If you're more interested in
exploring open source projects,

00:20:10.090 --> 00:20:13.520
there's a variety of open source
machine learning projects.

00:20:13.520 --> 00:20:15.280
TensorFlow is open source.

00:20:15.280 --> 00:20:18.010
Project Magenta, which is
the team I was on at Brain,

00:20:18.010 --> 00:20:21.880
has a lot of creativity
based, so music, art,

00:20:21.880 --> 00:20:24.110
generation-based models.

00:20:24.110 --> 00:20:26.620
And so you could provide
your own datasets.

00:20:26.620 --> 00:20:30.390
You can try to build your own
front end for these tools.

00:20:30.390 --> 00:20:34.168
And I think that would be
in general type of tools

00:20:34.168 --> 00:20:34.960
that you would use.

00:20:34.960 --> 00:20:36.460
Now if you're a
developer and you're

00:20:36.460 --> 00:20:38.740
just like, how can I
use machine learning,

00:20:38.740 --> 00:20:42.550
I think it's a matter of what
you're trying to do with it,

00:20:42.550 --> 00:20:46.220
but there's a whole range of
types of tools, let's say.

00:20:46.220 --> 00:20:48.350
So you could have
pretrained models,

00:20:48.350 --> 00:20:53.440
so as you would get from talking
to Google Assistant or Siri

00:20:53.440 --> 00:20:56.740
or Alexa, a lot of
this speech to text

00:20:56.740 --> 00:20:59.200
is ongoingly being
worked on, and so

00:20:59.200 --> 00:21:01.660
that would be use
case that you wouldn't

00:21:01.660 --> 00:21:03.100
need to build from scratch.

00:21:03.100 --> 00:21:06.590
You could use Google's
pretrained speech to text API,

00:21:06.590 --> 00:21:08.290
for example, to be
able to parse that.

00:21:08.290 --> 00:21:11.130
Or things like natural
language, or things that--

00:21:11.130 --> 00:21:14.080
general image recognition
type use cases,

00:21:14.080 --> 00:21:15.820
like maybe sentiments,
whether someone's

00:21:15.820 --> 00:21:17.470
happy or sad in a photo.

00:21:17.470 --> 00:21:19.540
Those are things that
are general enough

00:21:19.540 --> 00:21:23.765
that can be continually worked
on by these bigger companies.

00:21:23.765 --> 00:21:25.390
And if you want
something more custom--

00:21:25.390 --> 00:21:27.130
so if, say, you
have your own data,

00:21:27.130 --> 00:21:29.920
you have a list of
customers or you

00:21:29.920 --> 00:21:33.100
have a bunch of
retail data, you want

00:21:33.100 --> 00:21:35.158
to be able to train
your own custom model,

00:21:35.158 --> 00:21:37.450
that's where you use auto
machine learning, which still

00:21:37.450 --> 00:21:40.810
lets you customize a lot
more than you would with some

00:21:40.810 --> 00:21:43.660
of the pretrained APIs,
but at the same time

00:21:43.660 --> 00:21:47.200
you're not really doing much of
the machine learning algorithms

00:21:47.200 --> 00:21:48.200
yourself.

00:21:48.200 --> 00:21:50.470
Finally would be just,
you could also always

00:21:50.470 --> 00:21:51.650
code it up from scratch.

00:21:51.650 --> 00:21:54.310
So I think that there's a
whole range of different tools

00:21:54.310 --> 00:21:57.130
that you can use to get a
better understanding of machine

00:21:57.130 --> 00:21:59.590
learning, and I do
think that, as we've

00:21:59.590 --> 00:22:02.008
seen, as we've discussed
and as we've been explaining

00:22:02.008 --> 00:22:03.550
with the AI Impact
Challenge, I think

00:22:03.550 --> 00:22:06.460
having the right problem
also really matters if you're

00:22:06.460 --> 00:22:09.070
getting serious into using
machine learning for real world

00:22:09.070 --> 00:22:10.090
use cases.

00:22:10.090 --> 00:22:12.580
JON: So Sherol, we like to
do this thing on our podcast

00:22:12.580 --> 00:22:14.740
where we ask our guests,
what is something

00:22:14.740 --> 00:22:17.208
cool or awesome you've
seen done with AI or ML

00:22:17.208 --> 00:22:18.500
that you've seen done recently.

00:22:18.500 --> 00:22:19.990
So you want to
share that with us?

00:22:19.990 --> 00:22:22.157
SHEROL CHEN: So yeah, that's
a really good question.

00:22:22.157 --> 00:22:24.400
I would definitely say
one of the biggest things

00:22:24.400 --> 00:22:27.500
to pay attention to is AutoML.

00:22:27.500 --> 00:22:29.320
So for auto machine
learning, it's

00:22:29.320 --> 00:22:32.500
really a step in
between having the API

00:22:32.500 --> 00:22:35.620
or having the model already
pretrained and ready to go.

00:22:35.620 --> 00:22:37.900
You just give it inputs
and it gives you outputs.

00:22:37.900 --> 00:22:39.790
It's kind of the middle ground
between that and building

00:22:39.790 --> 00:22:40.910
everything from scratch.

00:22:40.910 --> 00:22:42.785
And so what you do with
auto machine learning

00:22:42.785 --> 00:22:46.030
is the ability to be able
to take data that you have

00:22:46.030 --> 00:22:47.830
and create a custom
prediction model that

00:22:47.830 --> 00:22:50.020
suits your needs specifically.

00:22:50.020 --> 00:22:51.820
Now, I definitely
think, as we talked

00:22:51.820 --> 00:22:54.280
about earlier with ethics,
that it is, in a sense,

00:22:54.280 --> 00:22:56.890
democratization,
but I don't think

00:22:56.890 --> 00:23:00.170
it replaces really understanding
how machine learning works.

00:23:00.170 --> 00:23:01.880
So while I think
it's a great tool

00:23:01.880 --> 00:23:04.510
and it's going to give a
lot of developers access

00:23:04.510 --> 00:23:07.750
to be able to use machine
learning in their applications

00:23:07.750 --> 00:23:09.730
or tools that they
want to build,

00:23:09.730 --> 00:23:12.490
I do think it's still
important to understand

00:23:12.490 --> 00:23:14.860
how you would do it by
yourself if you needed to.

00:23:14.860 --> 00:23:16.960
Because as much as
we want it to be

00:23:16.960 --> 00:23:19.510
easy to use, at the end
of the day, some of it

00:23:19.510 --> 00:23:22.640
is still being maintained
by a third party.

00:23:22.640 --> 00:23:24.110
So I do think it's magical.

00:23:24.110 --> 00:23:25.037
I think it's great.

00:23:25.037 --> 00:23:27.370
I think that there's a lot
of science behind that magic,

00:23:27.370 --> 00:23:29.080
and it's great to
cultivate a curiosity

00:23:29.080 --> 00:23:31.290
to understand how it all works.

00:23:31.290 --> 00:23:34.510
But yeah, I would say that
the coolest thing right now

00:23:34.510 --> 00:23:38.440
is this ability to just see
something like AutoML tables.

00:23:38.440 --> 00:23:40.260
I worked on the
promo video for it

00:23:40.260 --> 00:23:42.670
and I showed some of
my developer friends

00:23:42.670 --> 00:23:45.057
after it was out, and they
were just like, on one hand,

00:23:45.057 --> 00:23:47.140
it's like, yeah, of course
this should be a thing.

00:23:47.140 --> 00:23:49.372
Of course you should
be able to just select

00:23:49.372 --> 00:23:50.830
a column in your
spreadsheet and be

00:23:50.830 --> 00:23:53.530
able to predict the values
based off of the other columns.

00:23:53.530 --> 00:23:55.030
On the other hand,
it's like, wow, I

00:23:55.030 --> 00:23:57.230
can't believe this
is really happening.

00:23:57.230 --> 00:24:00.063
We're actually at this point
where we can see this stuff.

00:24:00.063 --> 00:24:01.230
So yeah, I think that that--

00:24:01.230 --> 00:24:04.280
I mean, there's so many other
