00:25:52.500 --> 00:25:56.100
You mentioned that you can run
App Engine Standard and Rails,

00:25:56.100 --> 00:25:57.598
so how do you handle migrations?

00:25:57.598 --> 00:25:59.640
AJA: So this is one of
those things that comes up

00:25:59.640 --> 00:26:03.340
every time we deal with
Ruby because Ruby has a web

00:26:03.340 --> 00:26:03.840
framework.

00:26:03.840 --> 00:26:05.640
There's multiple,
but a lot of folks

00:26:05.640 --> 00:26:08.612
use Rails or use Rails like
Frameworks based on rack.

00:26:08.612 --> 00:26:11.070
And we had to handle this when
we originally did App Engine

00:26:11.070 --> 00:26:13.607
Flex, but you also run into
similar stuff with Django,

00:26:13.607 --> 00:26:15.190
and someone on our
team has worked out

00:26:15.190 --> 00:26:16.320
how to solve that issue.

00:26:16.320 --> 00:26:18.060
But the answer to how
you run migrations

00:26:18.060 --> 00:26:20.618
is with a gem we have
called the App Engine Gem.

00:26:20.618 --> 00:26:22.410
There'll be a link to
it in the show notes.

00:26:22.410 --> 00:26:23.940
You install it
like any other gem,

00:26:23.940 --> 00:26:26.437
and it basically gives you
the ability to run Rake tasks.

00:26:26.437 --> 00:26:28.770
Rake, for those of you who
aren't familiar with the Ruby

00:26:28.770 --> 00:26:32.910
ecosystem, is like Make, but for
Ruby, so it starts with an R.

00:26:32.910 --> 00:26:36.030
And this allows you to run your
arbitrary Rake commands, things

00:26:36.030 --> 00:26:38.670
like Rake D Migrate,
Rake D Create,

00:26:38.670 --> 00:26:40.650
against your
production environment.

00:26:40.650 --> 00:26:42.750
And it does it
using Cloud Build,

00:26:42.750 --> 00:26:44.670
and it's really
awesome because it

00:26:44.670 --> 00:26:47.370
lets you use most of the same
workflow that you're used to,

00:26:47.370 --> 00:26:49.500
but it does things in
a secure and safe way.

00:26:49.500 --> 00:26:52.530
And it's fantastic and
it's pretty seamless.

00:26:52.530 --> 00:26:54.300
I've had really, really
good luck with it

00:26:54.300 --> 00:26:56.340
when I've been using App
Engine for applications

00:26:56.340 --> 00:26:57.030
and production.

00:26:57.030 --> 00:26:59.850
So that exists, and that
answers the question.

00:26:59.850 --> 00:27:01.230
Probably the most
common question

00:27:01.230 --> 00:27:04.350
we get about Rails + GCP is,
how do I handle migrations?

00:27:04.350 --> 00:27:06.360
And the answer is
the App Engine gem.

00:27:06.360 --> 00:27:08.190
